extractImageEquations -- finds implicit equations in a fixed degree for the image of a variety
**********************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        extractImageEquations(F, I, d)
        extractImageEquations T
  * Inputs:
      * T, a *note numerical interpolation table: NumericalInterpolationTable,,
        a numerical interpolation table for $F(V(I))$ of degree $d$
      * F, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, or *note list:
        (Macaulay2Doc)List,, or *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,,
        specifying a map
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, which is prime, specifying a
        source variety $V(I)$
      * d, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the argument of the Hilbert
        function of $F(V(I))$
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * AttemptZZ => ..., default value false
      * Threshold => ..., default value 5
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, of implicit degree d equations
        for $F(V(I))$

Description
===========

This method finds (approximate) implicit degree $d$ equations for the image of
a variety, by *note numerical interpolation: numericalHilbertFunction,. The
option AttemptZZ specifies whether to use the *note LLL: (LLLBases)LLL,
algorithm to compute "short" equations over *note ZZ: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,. The
default value is *note false: (Macaulay2Doc)false,.

If a numerical interpolation table has already been computed, then to avoid
repetitive calculation one may run this function with the interpolation table
as input.

For example, we determine the defining quadrics of the twisted cubic, as
follows.



+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = CC[s,t]                                              |
|                                                              |
|o1 = R                                                        |
|                                                              |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : F = basis(3, R)                                          |
|                                                              |
|o2 = | s3 s2t st2 t3 |                                        |
|                                                              |
|             1      4                                         |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R                                          |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : extractImageEquations(F, ideal 0_R, 2, AttemptZZ => true)|
|Sampling image points ...                                     |
|     -- used .00432569 seconds                                |
|Creating interpolation matrix ...                             |
|     -- used .00331869 seconds                                |
|Performing normalization preconditioning ...                  |
|     -- used .00132397 seconds                                |
|Computing numerical kernel ...                                |
|     -- used .000271717 seconds                               |
|                                                              |
|o3 = | y_1^2-y_0y_2 y_1y_2-y_0y_3 y_2^2-y_1y_3 |              |
|                                                              |
|                          1                   3               |
|o3 : Matrix (CC  [y ..y ])  <-- (CC  [y ..y ])                |
|               53  0   3           53  0   3                  |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+



Here is how to do the same computation symbolically.



+---------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : gens ker map(QQ[s,t], QQ[y_0..y_3], {s^3,s^2*t,s*t^2,t^3})|
|                                                               |
|o4 = | y_2^2-y_1y_3 y_1y_2-y_0y_3 y_1^2-y_0y_2 |               |
|                                                               |
|                        1                 3                    |
|o4 : Matrix (QQ[y ..y ])  <-- (QQ[y ..y ])                     |
|                 0   3             0   3                       |
+---------------------------------------------------------------+



We determine the $5$ Pl&uuml;cker quadrics defining the Grassmannian $Gr(3,5)$
of $P^2$'s in $P^4$, in the ambient space $P^9$.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : R = CC[x_(1,1)..x_(3,5)]; I = ideal 0_R;                                |
|                                                                             |
|o6 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : F = (minors(3, genericMatrix(R, 3, 5)))_*;                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : T = numericalHilbertFunction(F, I, 2, Verbose => false);                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : extractImageEquations(T, AttemptZZ => true)                             |
|                                                                             |
|o9 = | y_3y_5-y_2y_6+y_0y_9 y_2y_4-y_1y_5+y_0y_7 y_3y_4-y_1y_6+y_0y_8        |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     y_3y_7-y_2y_8+y_1y_9 y_6y_7-y_5y_8+y_4y_9 |                             |
|                                                                             |
|                          1                   5                              |
|o9 : Matrix (CC  [y ..y ])  <-- (CC  [y ..y ])                               |
|               53  0   9           53  0   9                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+



The option Threshold sets the threshold for rounding the interpolation matrix.
If this option has value $n$, then the interpolation matrix will be rounded to
$n$ decimal digits, after which LLL will be performed. The default value is
$5$.

